# XCU read
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# read is intrinsic (XCU 1.7). Input comes from files made by the test
# and is given to read by redirection, never by a terminal. IFS is
# restored after each use.

assertion  XCU.read.001
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/read.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/read.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       read reads one line and splits it on IFS (default blank, tab,
           newline) into the variables named, the last variable getting
           the rest of the line; exits zero.
test       t/001-split.sh

assertion  XCU.read.002
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With one variable the whole line is assigned with leading and
           trailing IFS white space removed.
test       t/002-single-variable.sh

assertion  XCU.read.003
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Variables left over after the fields run out are set to the
           empty string.
test       t/003-extra-variables.sh

assertion  XCU.read.004
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/read.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       Without -r a backslash escapes the next character (it is
           removed and the character is kept out of field splitting);
           with -r the backslash is kept as an ordinary character.
test       t/004-backslash.sh

assertion  XCU.read.005
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/read.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       Without -r a backslash before a newline continues the logical
           line on the next input line, and both are removed; with -r the
           line ends at the first newline and the backslash stays.
test       t/005-continuation.sh

assertion  XCU.read.006
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/read.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       Input that ends before a newline is seen still sets the
           variables from the bytes read and exits one; empty input sets
           the variable to the empty string and exits one.
test       t/006-eof.sh

assertion  XCU.read.007
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With IFS set to the empty string the whole line goes to the
           first variable unchanged and the other variables are set to
           the empty string.
test       t/007-empty-ifs.sh

assertion  XCU.read.008
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.5
class      required
since      1992
says       With a non-blank IFS character each occurrence separates a
           field, so adjacent delimiters give an empty field.
test       t/008-ifs-delimiters.sh

assertion  XCU.read.009
anchor     utilities/read.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      2024
says       read -d delim takes the single byte delim as the end of the
           logical line instead of a newline, and a newline inside the
           data is kept; the rest of the input stays for the next read.
test       t/009-delimiter.sh

assertion  XCU.read.010
anchor     utilities/read.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      2024
says       read -d with the empty string takes the null byte as the line
           delimiter.
test       t/010-null-delimiter.sh

assertion  XCU.read.011
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4
class      required
since      1992
says       read takes only the bytes of one line from a seekable input:
           a following command on the same open file sees the lines
           after it.
test       t/011-offset.sh

assertion  XCU.read.012
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Run in a subshell, read does not change the variables of the
           shell that started it.
test       t/012-subshell.sh

assertion  XCU.read.013
anchor     utilities/read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/read.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       When a variable cannot be set because it is read-only, read
           exits with a status greater than one.
test       t/013-readonly.sh
